'use strict';
// Study content. Loaded as a classic script before app.js; these top-level consts are shared globals.
//
// Japanese sentence markup (used in VOCAB and GRAMMAR examples, not in quizzes):
//   {漢字|かんじ}  ruby: base text and its reading. Every kanji must sit inside one of these.
//   [ ... ]      highlight: the vocab word or grammar point the sentence is showing.
// Run `python3 tools/check_content.py` after editing content.

/* ---------- Vocabulary ---------- */
// [written form, reading, meaning, group, [[polite example, translation, casual example], ...], frequency rank]
// Readings separated by ・ are alternatives (十: じゅう・とお). A leading ～ marks a counter or suffix (～本).
// Frequency rank: the word's rank among dictionary forms in the BCCWJ corpus (NINJAL's short- and long-unit
// frequency lists, free for educational use), 0 when it couldn't be matched. Rank ≤ 1000 shows a "Top 1000" badge.
// The N5 word list comes from Jonathan Waller's JLPT lists (tanos.co.uk, CC BY) via github.com/elzup/jlpt-word-list (MIT).
// The id is 'v:'+written form, so never change a written form (saved progress is keyed on it).
// Example sentences rotate on the front of review cards; each version must highlight the word exactly once.
// The polite version uses です/ます; the casual one says the same thing in plain form (だ, 食べる, ない？ ...).
// The casual version may be left out when the sentence reads the same in both registers.
